WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:08.519
Today I'll be presenting about big data, big
brain data science and predictive health analytics.

00:00:08.519 --> 00:00:14.879
So to begin with, I
want to show you a brief outline of what I

00:00:14.879 --> 00:00:18.740
would be covering the next 40 to 45 minutes
today.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.790
First of all, I will start
off with discussing some driving biomedical

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:27.470
and health challenges that lead to some of
the necessary

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:32.840
developments in the data science world, in
the computational statistics world.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:40.429
So then I will focus on identifying some of
the common characteristics that often times

00:00:40.429 --> 00:00:43.270
describe big brain data.

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.309
I
will explicitly define what I mean by data

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:48.079
science and predictive analytics.

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:54.399
And I will go over several case studies,
specifically in the domain of neurodegenerative

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:55.399
disorders.

00:00:55.399 --> 00:01:01.809
I'll show you one coapplication of data dashboarding,
using the software dashboard web app.

00:01:01.809 --> 00:01:09.060
And then finally, I'll present to you the
idea behind a really interesting new methodological

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.210
development,
something called compressive big data analytics,

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:18.250
that is intended to provide a canonical way
of mathematically

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:24.189
representing, statistically analyzing, and
computationally processing large complex heterogeneous

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:25.490
data sets.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:28.250
So that's the outline.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:35.450
So let's start off with some examples of driving
biomedical and health challenges.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.420
So this is a
recent study that we did a year and a half,

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:44.179
two years ago, looking at Alzheimer's disease,
specifically as a

00:01:44.179 --> 00:01:49.420
component of the vast spectrum of neurodegenerative
disorders.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:56.320
So in that specific study, we had about 800
Alzheimer's disease subjects coming from the

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:01.340
Alzheimer's disease neuroimaging initiative,
a large public-private

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:09.090
initiative to aggregate a very heterogeneous
multi-source, multi-institutional collection

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:16.230
of data for patients,
Alzheimer's disease patients, as well as asymptomatic

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.290
controls in these individuals that are kind
of transitioning

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.390
from being controls into being patients.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:27.010
Something called MCI, so mildly cognitively
impaired individuals.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:32.480
So in this specific study, we looked at the
three different cohorts, and what we did is

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:39.599
we extracted about 80, what
we called derived neuroimaging biomarkers.

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:44.209
And then additional 80 associated SNPs that
were highly associated

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:46.810
with the diagnoses.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:53.379
In this case, controls, MCIs, and ADs, in
that kind of progressive spectrum.

00:02:53.379 --> 00:03:00.150
So the images on
the left here illustrate some of the parcelations

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:03.370
that we did for the brain, and some of the
statistics that we extract,

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:09.670
and you can find more about that study in
that publication.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:17.510
So there is the recognition that when you
analyze biomedical health data, there is typically

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:22.560
three facets of
variability that one needs to be aware.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.610
The first one is obviously the phenotype,
which is the outward

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:33.430
manifestation of the form, shape, size, characterization,
of the specific individual, if you're doing

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:36.280
precision
medicine on an individual level.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:41.159
Or cohort-based science, atlas-based studies
and population-based studies.

00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:44.049
Or
you can be doing phenotyping based on various

00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:46.890
comorbid conditions.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:51.140
In addition to the phenotype, we have
obviously the nature, or the genome, that

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:55.810
plays role in everything that we look at,
in biomedical world.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.330
And then
there is also the nurture and the environment.

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:08.379
Now, across these three pillars, I guess,
of natural studies, chance plays a big role.

00:04:08.379 --> 00:04:12.990
So there is random effects,
there is noise that needs to be deal with.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:17.829
And one of the-- on the right side here, I
tried to indicate this notion that

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:25.479
most of the studies effectively resolve into
trying to tease out effective biological signal,

00:04:25.479 --> 00:04:31.039
relative to their natural
presence of biological noise, as well as noise

00:04:31.039 --> 00:04:35.229
that's technically introduced by the equipment,
by humans, by the

00:04:35.229 --> 00:04:37.020
processing, et cetera.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:44.080
So most of the studies that people do effectively
revolve around trying to maximize the information

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.910
content, or
signal, relative to the corresponding noise

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:51.990
that is unavoidable to a large extent.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.780
So in this next slide, I'm trying to
make the case for why should we look at big

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:57.780
data?

00:04:57.780 --> 00:04:58.780
And what is big data?

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.840
And what is standard data, or classical,
or small data.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:06.440
So in this left panel here, I have a representation
of a natural process here.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:10.440
So the native process
could be something that's inscribing in this

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:15.080
specific case, go blue here, with different
type of characteristics for

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:19.720
these individuals colors, orientations of
the subjects, and so forth.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:26.220
And on the extreme, on the right-hand side,
you have typical what a sample data set represents.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:31.360
It's a snapshot
that's pretending to be a proxy of the actual

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.229
population, which oftentimes is not completely
observable through a

00:05:35.229 --> 00:05:36.490
census.

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:42.610
So most of the time, we view these limited
process snapshots of the observations and

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:47.389
we try to make the
inference using data on the bottom right corner

00:05:47.389 --> 00:05:51.639
to infer the scripture that's on the bottom
left corner.

00:05:51.639 --> 00:05:56.949
Now big data is this in-between case, where
you're collecting, obviously not a census

00:05:56.949 --> 00:06:01.380
of the population, but you're
collecting sufficient amount of large-scale

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:05.069
information about the process, looking at
different directions, different

00:06:05.069 --> 00:06:08.130
scale, different modalities, et cetera.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:09.979
And you're trying to, again, do the same thing.

00:06:09.979 --> 00:06:15.560
You're trying to infer
characterization of computable phenotypes

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:21.450
on the actual native process, which is often
times not observable in its

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:22.450
entirety.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:26.699
So obviously there's pros and cons for looking
at sample-based inference statistics, versus

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:31.790
looking at big data
inference, where there's lots of challenges,

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:37.380
in terms of the harmonization, the aggregation,
the processing, et

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:38.380
cetera.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:42.050
So by the way, my colleague, Crystal, she
is going to be reviewing.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:46.020
If anybody has any questions, you can
post them in a chat and towards the end, we'll

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:49.699
probably have a chance to go over some questions
or comments

00:06:49.699 --> 00:06:53.610
that may arise during the presentation.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:07:00.190
So in this next slide, I'm showing you what
we call the constructive definition of big

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:01.830
biomedical data.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:05.319
So big data
obviously was first defined by IBM, in terms

00:07:05.319 --> 00:07:08.580
of the four Vs, about a decade ago.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:14.610
They recognized that the volume,
the variety, the speed at which data changes,

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:18.020
and the veracity, are important characteristics
and they call these

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:19.020
4Vs.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:23.550
Now these four Vs are nowadays extended to
seven Vs and eight Vs. There is more v-words

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:27.450
that describe
the complexities that have to do with big

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:28.669
data.

00:07:28.669 --> 00:07:34.160
Now last year, what we did is we tried to
come up with a more structured, constructive

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:38.930
definition that identifies very
specifically what are the challenges and what

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:43.090
are the needs for tools, software, and services
that are very specific

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:47.490
to these what we call big biomedical data,
big brain data, et cetera.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:50.050
So the size is obviously one of these
characteristics.

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:53.090
It's one of the paramount characteristics,
but we don't stop there.

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:59.590
There is complexities with the data that requires
special type of data handlers, wranglers,

00:07:59.590 --> 00:08:03.669
that allow us to
manipulate heterogeneous data elements.

00:08:03.669 --> 00:08:07.150
Then you have data incongruency.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:13.789
The data will not be homologous
between subjects, between sites that are acquiring

00:08:13.789 --> 00:08:14.789
the data, and so forth.

00:08:14.789 --> 00:08:21.050
So sampling rates, so there's a lot of
tools that are necessary and techniques to

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:22.979
deal with this incongruency.

00:08:22.979 --> 00:08:27.909
Another very important characteristic of big
data is that it often times arrives from multiple

00:08:27.909 --> 00:08:29.409
sources.

00:08:29.409 --> 00:08:33.560
So the
transfer, the joint modeling and processing

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:38.710
of these disparate elements is a huge barrier
currently.

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:42.020
then you can
be looking at the same process, the same phenomenon.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:46.360
For example, the human brain, at multiple
scales.

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:51.350
Starting on the top, at the macroscopic level,
you have typically resolution of the brain

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:52.620
at about 1 millimeter.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.660
And
then you can go down to cryotomographic data,

00:08:55.660 --> 00:09:01.090
where you can have microscopic, or mesoscale.

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:05.950
And it you can go down to the nanoscale, or
a single channel recordings, where you are

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:07.850
on the micron level.

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:10.210
So
you're looking at exactly the same substrate,

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:13.470
exactly the same process, but you're looking
at different resolutions.

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:18.860
So the multi-scale aspect of big brain data
is very important.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:20.971
And last, but certainly not least, is the
fact that big

00:09:20.971 --> 00:09:24.720
data is always, always incomplete.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:29.870
So mechanisms of dealing with that incompleteness,
our group and other groups are developing

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:32.230
batch mode
techniques.

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:39.150
Because often times, if you have, as soon
as you cross the 10 to 100,000 cases, you

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:42.680
cannot-- it's
simply computationally impractical with current

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:47.460
technology, to do holistic, what's called
imputation.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:52.440
To obtain
complete snapshots of the data that can be

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:57.070
processed using more traditional techniques.

00:09:57.070 --> 00:10:00.530
So let's go ahead and define specifically
what we mean by data science and predictive

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:03.820
analytics, because these
terms are important.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:09.650
So in my world, data science is defined as
this emerging, extremely transdisciplinary

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:14.130
area,
which bridges the theoretical, the computational,

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:18.460
the experimental, and then the biosocial or
the biomedical areas.

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:23.970
Data science deals with these characteristics
that I defined earlier, these enormous amounts

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:27.940
of complex
incongruent, heterogeneous dynamically changing

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:32.590
data that arise from multiple streams or multiple
sources.

00:10:32.590 --> 00:10:38.800
Data science always develops methods, tests
methods, validates methods, shares methods,

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:45.630
tools, services, and
capabilities, to enable ingestion of these

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:50.640
complex data sets, and applying completely
automated or semiautomated

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:55.890
decision support methods and systems.

00:10:55.890 --> 00:10:59.850
Predictive analytics is the process that we
use for utilizing

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:05.580
advanced mathematical formalizations, powerful
statistical algorithms, and efficient software

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:10.100
tools-- as well as
services-- to number one, represent, number

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:15.850
two, interrogate, and number three, interpret,
complex data sets.

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:18.770
So
predictive analytics focuses on identifying

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:26.180
trends, clusters, motifs, prognostication
of process behaviors, either

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:33.110
across time, space, within the range and outside
of the range of the originally observed data.

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:38.120
And again, I want to remind you of the little
cartoon that I had early on, about the complete

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:42.390
population data, the go
blue schematic versus the big data set and

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:46.740
the small samples of traditional samples.

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:50.150
Because this notion of
observing data outside of range and inside

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:54.440
of the range is very important so, by the
way, these two definitions

00:11:54.440 --> 00:12:00.000
come up from on-- they are directly coming
from an upcoming Springer book on the topic

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.700
of data science and
predictive analytics.

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:10.150
So this is a diagram that illustrates a typical
process when you are analyzing big brain data.

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:13.690
The typical process
starts with collecting the data that comes

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:17.630
as raw observations, bits, bytes, that are
typically aggregated.

00:12:17.630 --> 00:12:21.160
You have
data scrubbing, data cleaning, manipulations,

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:27.110
and so forth, that need to be conducted first,
before some kind of an

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:30.270
information gets extracted out of these data.

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:32.360
For instance, summary statistics.

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:38.090
In our case, we take the raw imaging, magnetic
resonance imaging scans, for example.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:43.200
And then we remove the
non-brain tissue.

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:47.990
Then we model the cortical surface of the
brain, which is the boundary between the gray

00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:51.320
matter
and the cerebrospinal fluid, using mathematical

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:53.610
two-dimensional manifolds.

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:55.350
So that's in the information content.

00:12:55.350 --> 00:13:02.390
And then the next step, the next column here
is the knowledge generation phase, at which

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:07.010
you begin to look at
these models, and you obtain summary statistics.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:10.320
For example, you can be computing surface
areas, you can be

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:15.310
computing shape indices, you can be computing
fractal dimension, you can be computing a

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:19.610
variety of
morphometry measures for each of these areas.

00:13:19.610 --> 00:13:23.540
And then, based on these, you typically develop
learning

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:31.520
algorithms, or classifiers, that can lead
to some action that obviously, is in our world,

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:38.850
pertains to mediating normal
development or various heterogeneous brain

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:41.820
disorders.

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:45.430
So I want to start with some examples now,
or case studies.

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:48.600
So in the first case study, this is a case
study of

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:50.680
amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:55.060
A very debilitating, progressive neurodegenerative
disorders.

00:13:55.060 --> 00:14:00.670
So we wanted to
identify predictive mechanisms to detect,

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:06.880
track, and prognosticate the progression of
ALS across time.

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:09.330
Now
although ALS is not extremely common-- there

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:14.270
is about 30,000 to 50,000 cases in the US--
it is very debilitating.

00:14:14.270 --> 00:14:20.120
It's also called Lou Gehrig's disease, and
Stephen Hawking is one of those prominent,

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:24.700
outspoken obviously
patients with ALS.

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:28.940
So in this specific case, we had a very heterogeneous
data set.

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:33.700
That started again, over 100 different features
were recorded, for a number of subjects.

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:39.940
They included demographic data, clinical data,
what are called ALSFRS,

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:45.520
these amyotrophic lateral sclerosis functional
rating scale clinical measures.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:51.200
There were, in addition, adverse
events included with some of the patients.

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:55.690
And we started with about 8,600 cases.

00:14:55.690 --> 00:15:02.080
And in the process of stratifying
the data and manipulating the data to the

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:08.330
point where it becomes a single computable
object that can be jointly

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:15.880
monitored and interrogated, the data was reduced
to about 2,400 cases, simply to avoid missingness,

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:19.090
to avoid
incongruencies, and so forth.

00:15:19.090 --> 00:15:27.370
And then we looked in this specific case,
trying to predict the progression of the disease,

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:33.270
in terms of monitoring
these ALSFRS metrics, very specifically finding

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:34.590
the slope.

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:37.630
So we are given the information at baseline,
zero

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:42.530
month, and then at month three, one quarter
later.

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:45.410
We're given this information and we are asked
to predict a 12-

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:47.410
month follow up.

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:52.110
Where is the ALSFRS clinical score for these
patients going to be?

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:57.030
So we developed this fairly
elaborate protocol that's shown in the left,

00:15:57.030 --> 00:16:01.170
that illustrates very concretely, how we go
from the original

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:07.430
observations, all the way down to the predictions
and then the assessment of these predictions,

00:16:07.430 --> 00:16:13.470
in terms of
various quantitative evaluation metrics.

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:20.310
The panel on the left shows you the variable
importance plot, that directly identifies

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:26.200
features that are important in
that prediction of the 12-month follow up

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:30.140
from the first three months of observations.

00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:35.550
And then the table on the
bottom right illustrates three different types

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:39.780
of classifiers, linear, model-based techniques
and then three modelfree

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:45.350
machine learning based techniques, using random
forest, based in adaptive regression trees,

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:47.230
and a
superlative method.

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:52.200
And then the rows in the table illustrate
three very specific metrics that quantify

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:53.220
the
performance.

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:59.120
One is called the R-squared metric, that identifies
the relative model performance.

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:01.200
The root means square error.

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:10.060
And then the correlation between the actual
observed ALSFRS slope change between the first

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:13.430
three months and
the 12-month, and the actual predicted, by

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:18.560
the corresponding method slope change.

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:25.069
And you can see that we're
reaching about 0.5 or so prediction accuracy.

00:17:25.069 --> 00:17:30.250
The second neurodegenerative study that I
want to point out is a Parkinson's disease

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:31.750
study.

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:35.350
So there is something
very similar for Parkinson's as there is for

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:36.630
ALS.

00:17:36.630 --> 00:17:40.310
There is clinical scores called uniform Parkinson's
disease rating

00:17:40.310 --> 00:17:43.120
scale assessments.

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:51.560
And again, we are trying to predict the progression
of the clinical diagnosis of the patients.

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:56.150
Can we separate, for instance, patients from
age match controls?

00:17:56.150 --> 00:18:03.110
With and without using the corresponding
UPDRS clinical assessments that the physicians

00:18:03.110 --> 00:18:05.580
base their diagnoses on?

00:18:05.580 --> 00:18:09.770
So the table on the bottom illustrates, again,
a little bit about the cohort.

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:15.680
There is genetics data, there is imaging
data, there is three different cohorts.

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:20.920
Asymptomatic controls or their controls, the
patients, the PD patients, and

00:18:20.920 --> 00:18:25.420
then there is these patients that are in between
they're called SWEDDs, subjects without evidence

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:27.500
of
dopaminergic deficit.

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:35.800
So if you look at their imaging scans, the
radiologist can not clearly identify a Parkinson's

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:40.630
disease deficit, in terms of dopamine change.

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:43.230
So for that specific study we again, developed
an end-to-end protocol that starts with the

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:44.230
data aggregation on the
So for that specific study we again, developed

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:47.080
an end-to-end protocol that starts with the
data aggregation on the

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:48.080
very left.

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:53.830
It goes through the data harmonization, aggregation
of all these different modalities of the data

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:55.420
coming
from the multiple sources.

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:59.360
They're very incongruent, they cannot not
be represented in the same function or

00:18:59.360 --> 00:19:00.360
bases.

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:06.450
Then we did the cleaning, the imputation,
the wrangling of the data.

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:10.970
And then because the cohorts, in the previous
slide, it was shown that the sizes of the

00:19:10.970 --> 00:19:14.980
three different cohorts that we are studying
are not very balanced.

00:19:14.980 --> 00:19:18.060
So for
example, the patients outnumbered controls

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:19.420
three to one.

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:25.180
There's a potential bias that may be introduced
by the

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:26.570
patient cohort.

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:34.160
So we used synthetic minority oversampling
method, which is a statistical technique that

00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:37.900
allow us to rebalance--
approximately, not exactly, but approximately--

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:40.300
the sample size of the three cohorts.

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:43.250
So that we avoid potential
bias.

00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:49.060
And then we did both, just like in the ALS
world, we did both model-based, for example,

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:53.460
linear model and
model-free classification of all the patients.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:57.500
And then we did predictions and we validated
the results.

00:19:57.500 --> 00:20:02.770
The workflow diagram that's in the bottom
left corner here, shows you the end-to-end

00:20:02.770 --> 00:20:07.050
protocol, the data goes
essentially over 80 to 90 different processing

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:14.020
steps to go from its raw aggregated state,
into some of these cortical

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:15.770
surface maps and parcelated maps.

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:21.620
And statistical signature vectors is what
we call these numbers that drive the

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:24.190
actual classification at the end.

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:29.090
And on the right hand side you have, again,
a variable importance plot, that

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:32.570
illustrates which feature, and you can see
here, there is demographic features, like

00:20:32.570 --> 00:20:33.570
age.

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:37.890
There's imaging features,
like the left superior occipital gyrus surface

00:20:37.890 --> 00:20:39.890
area is one of these features.

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:46.660
You have clinical measures, the UPDRS part
one was highly predictive of the diagnostic

00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:48.940
outcome of the subjects,
et cetera.

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:53.200
And you can see in the bottom right table,
again, some stunning statistics.

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:56.730
And these are certainly not
surprising, adaptive boosting and support

00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:58.450
vector machine classifiers.

00:20:58.450 --> 00:21:05.420
We're able to discriminate between the
different diagnostic cohorts incredibly well.

00:21:05.420 --> 00:21:10.310
Now this was simply because we use the actual
UPDRS, the uniform

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:16.140
Parkinson's disease rating scale measures,
which the physicians themselves use in the

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:18.480
determination of a
diagnosis.

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:28.220
When we exclude these clinical UPDRS biomarkers,
our classification goes down to about 80%,

00:21:28.220 --> 00:21:30.090
which is a
substantial decrease.

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:36.820
However, it is still pretty impactful and
a reliable indication that machine learning

00:21:36.820 --> 00:21:41.670
methods
can be used to do fairly powerful predictive

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:43.770
analytics.

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:46.760
So in this specific case study, just to summarize,
we have

00:21:46.760 --> 00:21:53.700
developed an end-to-end big brain data analytical
protocol that can jointly model, represent,

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:58.140
and interrogate
imaging, genetic, clinical, and demographic

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:02.920
data, to assess the risk for Parkinson's disease.

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:07.050
The source code is
available on this GitHub site, under the predictive

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:11.890
big data analytics software project.

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:13.970
Now let me follow up with something very recent.

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:19.830
So this is hot off the presses, they say,
although it's not peer

00:22:19.830 --> 00:22:20.830
reviewed.

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:27.320
It is a very, very interesting new study,
looking at Parkinson's disease patients falls.

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:32.310
Can you predict
physical injury from falling in Parkinson's

00:22:32.310 --> 00:22:34.210
disease?

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:37.110
So in Parkinson's disease there is two clinical
phenotypes.

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:42.810
There is tremor dominance, with people that
have limbs that basically shake and the second

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:50.770
type is called gait
instability and posture instability, or posture

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:52.400
instability and gait disorder.

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:56.320
So these are freezing of gait and various
other disorders.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:23:02.850
So in this specific case, we were interested
in doing three very specific challenges.

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:06.850
The first one is, we had data
from two different sources, here at the University

00:23:06.850 --> 00:23:10.070
of Michigan, we have a Udall Center for Excellence
in

00:23:10.070 --> 00:23:11.490
Parkinson's Disease.

00:23:11.490 --> 00:23:17.390
A U50 grant, funded by the National Institute
of Health, where we are collecting a lot of

00:23:17.390 --> 00:23:19.650
data
on Parkinson's patients.

00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:23.650
And a colleague of ours at Tel Aviv University
collected, completely independently, a very

00:23:23.650 --> 00:23:28.720
similar data set, again studying the same
cohort, not the same individuals, the same

00:23:28.720 --> 00:23:31.990
cohort, of PD subjects.

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:37.070
So the first challenge in this study was to
try to harmonize and aggregate these multi-source

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:39.300
and complex data
set.

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:43.620
The second challenge was to identify what
are the clinically relevant features that

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:48.050
are associated with very
specific clinical traits?

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:51.100
For example, falls.

00:23:51.100 --> 00:23:54.590
And the third one is trying to predict now,
once you have the feature

00:23:54.590 --> 00:24:00.100
selection, can you predict falls in these
Parkinson's disease using, again, model-based

00:24:00.100 --> 00:24:02.720
or model-free machine
learning methods?

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:06.270
So the study design is illustrated in this
diagram.

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:11.150
First of all, we did this independently, study
the Udall University of Michigan data set.

00:24:11.150 --> 00:24:15.020
We did the feature selection,
we did the modeling, we did what's called

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:18.590
statistical internal cross-validation of the
results.

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:22.040
And we did this
independently for the two data sets, the Tel

00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:24.140
Aviv University data set and Udall.

00:24:24.140 --> 00:24:28.230
Then in the second phase, we
actually aggregated the two data set and then

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:33.220
we did the modeling and the cross validation
on the aggregate data

00:24:33.220 --> 00:24:40.940
set, as well as we did an external out-of-bag
training on one of the data sets.

00:24:40.940 --> 00:24:46.920
And testing, or assessment, on the
other.

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:49.760
So the results were fairly encouraging.

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:56.430
We have, as I mentioned, missing data is one
of these key challenges that

00:24:56.430 --> 00:25:00.730
characterizes big biomedical data and big
brain data, in specific.

00:25:00.730 --> 00:25:07.180
So this diagram on the chart on the left corner
here, shows you the missing pattern of the

00:25:07.180 --> 00:25:08.180
data.

00:25:08.180 --> 00:25:12.740
And on the right, on the top right, I can
show you some of the

00:25:12.740 --> 00:25:20.090
differences in various, in four clinically
relevant variables, like tremor score, or

00:25:20.090 --> 00:25:25.390
posture instability and gait disorder
score, and various other metrics, between

00:25:25.390 --> 00:25:31.600
the fallers and the non-fallers, indicated
with purple and yellow colors.

00:25:31.600 --> 00:25:36.960
And then, one of the big challenges that occurred
in this case study, which is certainly very

00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:40.150
common when you do
big biomedical data, is the fact that the

00:25:40.150 --> 00:25:45.930
data will not be congruent or the statistically
aggregatable.

00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:49.400
So the diagram
that you see, the histogram plot on the bottom

00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:55.650
left corner, illustrates the very substantial
amount of difference

00:25:55.650 --> 00:26:02.670
between the distributions of various features
between the Michigan and the Tel-Aviv data.

00:26:02.670 --> 00:26:08.300
So everything that exceeds that threshold,
that red curve, that I hope is visible here,

00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:15.790
the horizontal red curve, that
is inverse log scale of the statistical significance

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:18.790
on the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test, between different
features.

00:26:18.790 --> 00:26:21.470
And
you can see about half the features are not

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:25.800
statistically distinct between the two populations,
but a huge number

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:31.360
of features are very, very distinct between
the two university databases.

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:33.640
So how can this be explained?

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:37.690
How can
these be handled to allow us to literally

00:26:37.690 --> 00:26:42.390
aggregate and learn from a bigger data set?

00:26:42.390 --> 00:26:47.970
OK, so we move forward, we dealt with these
challenges, and I just want to show you some

00:26:47.970 --> 00:26:48.970
of the results.

00:26:48.970 --> 00:26:51.600
So we
did the feature selection, by two different,

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:52.640
very interesting methods.

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:55.280
One is called a random forest feature
selection.

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:59.770
And you can see, in the middle column here,
the feature name, the variable names.

00:26:59.770 --> 00:27:05.181
And then right next to it, the frequency of
how frequently, and how common was it, how

00:27:05.181 --> 00:27:10.750
important was it, in the
prediction of the clinically relevant outcome?

00:27:10.750 --> 00:27:13.480
In this case, falling or not-falling.

00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:18.559
And then knockoff filter, in the
second column in this table on the right,

00:27:18.559 --> 00:27:22.430
illustrates a controlled variable selection,
where you have a mechanism of

00:27:22.430 --> 00:27:26.490
controlling the false positive rate of selecting
these features.

00:27:26.490 --> 00:27:31.890
And you can see that seven of these features
are commonly chosen by the two completely

00:27:31.890 --> 00:27:33.750
independent
strategies.

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:40.090
So that's a very strong indicator that we
might have zeroed down on which observations,

00:27:40.090 --> 00:27:44.450
which data
elements, would be highly predictive or associated

00:27:44.450 --> 00:27:46.940
with falls in Parkinson's.

00:27:46.940 --> 00:27:51.480
The bottom right table that's shown
here in the corner, illustrates a variety

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:56.460
of metrics from model-based logistic regression
of identifying and fallers

00:27:56.460 --> 00:28:02.070
from non-fallers, to random forest, adaptive
boosting, HG boosting, a number of alternative

00:28:02.070 --> 00:28:04.850
machine learning
based methods.

00:28:04.850 --> 00:28:11.170
And you can see that one of the best classifier,
in this case, random forest achieves accuracy

00:28:11.170 --> 00:28:17.400
of about 80%, with a
fairly high specificity of about 0.87.

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:21.400
And sensitivity, not as good, but about 0.7.

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:24.250
So this is, again, fairly encouraging.

00:28:24.250 --> 00:28:28.910
Now once you have that you can, obviously,
make recommendations to physicians, families,

00:28:28.910 --> 00:28:33.700
caregivers, and so
forth, about what to watch for or what to

00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:34.700
look at.

00:28:34.700 --> 00:28:40.230
Now in this next case study, we looked at
a more generic population, almost looking

00:28:40.230 --> 00:28:41.420
at a census.

00:28:41.420 --> 00:28:46.780
So this data
comes from the United Kingdom, this is the

00:28:46.780 --> 00:28:48.090
UK Biobank.

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:50.840
In this specific case study, we wanted to
discriminate

00:28:50.840 --> 00:28:57.730
between healthy patients and multiple comorbid--
patients with multiple comorbid conditions.

00:28:57.730 --> 00:29:02.200
Now in the UK
Biobank data, what is interesting is that

00:29:02.200 --> 00:29:07.059
they try to take a snapshot of the entire
population, in this case.

00:29:07.059 --> 00:29:10.309
And this
shows a little bit about the data.

00:29:10.309 --> 00:29:14.790
So over half a million cases, over 4,000 features.

00:29:14.790 --> 00:29:19.720
They do include imaging data,
not for all the subjects, but for many subjects,

00:29:19.720 --> 00:29:22.710
about 10,000 subjects, they have structural
and functional imaging

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:24.130
and diffusion imaging data.

00:29:24.130 --> 00:29:26.080
They have genetics data and so forth.

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:29.060
So it was a very challenging study.

00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:32.920
And the URL links that are provided here,
in the bottom right corner might be

00:29:32.920 --> 00:29:34.601
useful, if you want to follow up with this.

00:29:34.601 --> 00:29:38.230
This is all public data, you can request data.

00:29:38.230 --> 00:29:41.640
So just to show some of the
complexities here, right, so we looked at

00:29:41.640 --> 00:29:44.490
the features, these literally 4,100 features.

00:29:44.490 --> 00:29:45.910
And we only looked at the
complexities here, right, so we looked at

00:29:45.910 --> 00:29:46.910
the features, these literally 4,100 features.

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:49.870
And we only looked at the
ones that are highly observed.

00:29:49.870 --> 00:29:54.720
So more than 30% of the cases having observed
data.

00:29:54.720 --> 00:30:00.570
And this is just a plot to
illustrate the missingness rate.

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:07.140
So it turns out that in our situation, we
essentially wanted to zero down on the subjects

00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:11.550
that had imaging data,
because we know really well how to do computational

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:12.860
neuroscience.

00:30:12.860 --> 00:30:17.010
We know how to obtain these derived
neuroimaging signature factors.

00:30:17.010 --> 00:30:22.710
So for these 10,000 subjects, as you can see,
9,900 or so cases, we looked at

00:30:22.710 --> 00:30:27.950
the highly observed clinical phenotypes about
these 1,500 or so features.

00:30:27.950 --> 00:30:30.970
And we wanted to examine what is the
missing pattern?

00:30:30.970 --> 00:30:32.050
What features are missing?

00:30:32.050 --> 00:30:33.050
What is the rate of missing?

00:30:33.050 --> 00:30:36.930
And as you can see here, there is a very high
rate of missingness.

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:40.630
So we needed to develop techniques to deal
with that missingness.

00:30:40.630 --> 00:30:44.770
The missingness is obviously not random, you
can see from that pattern.

00:30:44.770 --> 00:30:49.940
So innovative
statistical methods are required to be able

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:54.770
to preprocess these specific data set to the
point where it can become,

00:30:54.770 --> 00:31:01.940
again, a computable data object, that we can
interrogate, either with or without model-based

00:31:01.940 --> 00:31:03.330
techniques.

00:31:03.330 --> 00:31:05.240
So a little bit about the protocol.

00:31:05.240 --> 00:31:11.740
So we developed this pipeline workflow, that
shows on a very high level here, the

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:17.330
process is that the imaging data goes through
from volume preprocessing to parcelation to

00:31:17.330 --> 00:31:21.740
computational, the
morphometry measures, all the way up to the

00:31:21.740 --> 00:31:28.010
result aggregation into these spreadsheets
or computable data

00:31:28.010 --> 00:31:30.720
objects.

00:31:30.720 --> 00:31:35.410
So not everything-- when you do computing
on this kind of scale, not everything is going

00:31:35.410 --> 00:31:36.410
to be perfect.

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:39.010
You're
going to have some successes and some failures.

00:31:39.010 --> 00:31:42.480
So I'm just showing you here, that when you
do a computing on

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:44.590
this scale, things are not always going to
be right.

00:31:44.590 --> 00:31:57.170
So in this specific case, we have 99% of the--
it says, 8,348

00:31:57.170 --> 00:32:03.700
compute jobs were done, at this specific phase
of the pipeline workflow. 99% of them are

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:04.700
complete.

00:32:04.700 --> 00:32:09.600
One was
canceled and 469 instances of the calculations

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:10.600
failed.

00:32:10.600 --> 00:32:14.590
So we can track down, it is very important,
through the LONI pipeline environment, to

00:32:14.590 --> 00:32:20.290
be able to track precisely
what steps in what cases fail, so that you

00:32:20.290 --> 00:32:23.380
can remedy and examine if there is anything
common for them.

00:32:23.380 --> 00:32:26.000
Now
obviously, your inference needs to be based

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:29.120
on the complete cases, going forward.

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:33.890
A little bit about the pipeline work environment
that we've been developing at LONI, and at

00:32:33.890 --> 00:32:38.880
the INI at USC, this has
been a project that, again, has started as

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:44.260
is being funded as a P41 project, part of
the National Institute of Health.

00:32:44.260 --> 00:32:50.320
It allows users to design, on their client
machine, these complicated protocols, so that

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:55.210
the calculations can be
outsourced to an external pipeline distributed

00:32:55.210 --> 00:33:01.330
server, where the calculations can be parallelized
on complex

00:33:01.330 --> 00:33:03.320
computational platforms.

00:33:03.320 --> 00:33:11.860
So the user doesn't have to worry about managing
of the data node assignments, job

00:33:11.860 --> 00:33:14.309
tracking, aggregation of results, and so forth.

00:33:14.309 --> 00:33:18.000
This is all kind of managed by the distributed
pipeline server on the background.

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:23.450
The user only worries about the
front-end design of the actual scientific

00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:27.490
protocol, the algorithm, the pipeline workflow.

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:31.910
That's what we do, and then
our colleagues at IT and the pipeline team

00:33:31.910 --> 00:33:36.410
actually worry about all the back-end things
that are, indeed, very

00:33:36.410 --> 00:33:40.700
important, for these types of calculations.

00:33:40.700 --> 00:33:47.740
So this is a slide that shows you a tool that
we developed as part of the Big Data Discovery

00:33:47.740 --> 00:33:50.000
Science, BD2K center.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:53.840
A couple of years ago, it's called the Big
Data Dashboard.

00:33:53.840 --> 00:33:59.140
It allows you to essentially query databases,
multivariate

00:33:59.140 --> 00:34:05.200
multi-source databases, without writing a
single line of SQL or any database language

00:34:05.200 --> 00:34:12.220
code, to essentially
visually generate a collage of images, each

00:34:12.220 --> 00:34:16.530
image representing one or multiple variables.

00:34:16.530 --> 00:34:21.980
And then all of these
charts are interactive, so they are like trellis

00:34:21.980 --> 00:34:22.980
charts.

00:34:22.980 --> 00:34:28.310
You can narrow the scope of the states, or
for example, you can select states, or you

00:34:28.310 --> 00:34:33.000
can select, in this case, this
is the histogram of the average commute time

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:34.590
for these individuals.

00:34:34.590 --> 00:34:38.240
So you can manipulate any one of these
charts, and as you manipulate one of them,

00:34:38.240 --> 00:34:42.929
all the other charts morph to reflect the
subsetting, to reflect the

00:34:42.929 --> 00:34:48.780
stratification that the user introduces in
one or several of these charts.

00:34:48.780 --> 00:34:54.929
So during that interactive, visual-based
data interrogation, you are able to formulate

00:34:54.929 --> 00:35:00.119
interesting research hypotheses about what
might be the multivariate

00:35:00.119 --> 00:35:03.940
intricate relations that are not a priori
known.

00:35:03.940 --> 00:35:07.680
So we can generate hypotheses that can obviously
be tested

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:12.920
through rigorous computational mechanisms.

00:35:12.920 --> 00:35:18.170
So in this example that I just show you, we
used data from the Center for Medicare Services,

00:35:18.170 --> 00:35:20.650
the CMS, on the
Medicare services.

00:35:20.650 --> 00:35:26.940
We used data from the US Census Bureau and
the Bureau of Labor Statistics, just to illustrate

00:35:26.940 --> 00:35:33.210
how data can be pulled from multiple incongruent,
in this case federal databases, but they're

00:35:33.210 --> 00:35:36.470
not designed to
necessarily interoperate.

00:35:36.470 --> 00:35:41.470
So we will demonstrate how the data can be
aggregated and therefore interrogated real

00:35:41.470 --> 00:35:45.720
time, through visual analytic concepts.

00:35:45.720 --> 00:35:52.520
All right, so in the last few minutes, I just
want to tell you about this compressive big

00:35:52.520 --> 00:35:57.960
data analytics strategy that
our group is developing.

00:35:57.960 --> 00:36:05.760
So a little bit of motivation here, there
is no current mathematical foundation of how

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:08.040
to
analyze big data.

00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:14.600
The science, the big data science is not developed.

00:36:14.600 --> 00:36:21.350
So we have looked at, again, the big data
characteristics, and we have introduced this

00:36:21.350 --> 00:36:24.850
strategy that allows us to do a stochastic-based
inference

00:36:24.850 --> 00:36:32.520
independent of the very specific case-based
data characteristics.

00:36:32.520 --> 00:36:34.970
As long as the data has the six dimensions
that I

00:36:34.970 --> 00:36:42.619
showed in one of my earlier slides, a large
size, coming from multiple sources, incongruency,

00:36:42.619 --> 00:36:46.840
multi-scale,
incomplete, and so forth.

00:36:46.840 --> 00:36:52.800
We are developing this iterative, statistical
stochastic algorithm that will allow us to

00:36:52.800 --> 00:36:57.900
generically process that data in
such a way that we generate subsamples from

00:36:57.900 --> 00:37:01.590
the big data, then we use model-based or model-free
techniques

00:37:01.590 --> 00:37:12.680
to analyze these samples, compute various
likelihoods, estimates, relations, or qualitative

00:37:12.680 --> 00:37:15.710
or quantitative relations
between variables.

00:37:15.710 --> 00:37:21.270
We repeat this process multiple times, and
we effectively look at the asymptotics.

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:26.810
We look at
the ergodic properties of these algorithms.

00:37:26.810 --> 00:37:29.960
So this diagram illustrates a little bit of
process.

00:37:29.960 --> 00:37:33.650
It's a eight-step process, very, very generic.

00:37:33.650 --> 00:37:37.430
It doesn't deal with
brain data, it doesn't deal with neurodegenerative

00:37:37.430 --> 00:37:38.430
data.

00:37:38.430 --> 00:37:42.080
It deals with any data set that has the six
dimensions that I

00:37:42.080 --> 00:37:44.660
outlined earlier about big biomedical data
sets.

00:37:44.660 --> 00:37:48.190
So the first step is obviously the pre-processing
cleaning step.

00:37:48.190 --> 00:37:56.220
Eliminating very strong dependencies, recasting
data type point necessary, dealing with missingness,

00:37:56.220 --> 00:37:57.880
and so
forth.

00:37:57.880 --> 00:38:05.330
In step two, it does the data harmonization,
the data set-- just like in the Udall Michigan

00:38:05.330 --> 00:38:09.270
versus Tel Aviv
Parkinson's disease falls data set that I

00:38:09.270 --> 00:38:14.740
showed you, there has to be a data aggregation
in harmonization step.

00:38:14.740 --> 00:38:20.270
In the second step, we do the variable selection
and so forth.

00:38:20.270 --> 00:38:23.810
And then we go through the iterative CBDA,
the

00:38:23.810 --> 00:38:31.910
compressive big data analytics iterative protocol,
where we randomly sample, in step four, a

00:38:31.910 --> 00:38:33.430
decent set.

00:38:33.430 --> 00:38:36.360
Then we
do the imputation, scaling, rebalancing, which

00:38:36.360 --> 00:38:39.520
I mentioned earlier as well, when we did the
Parkinson's disease

00:38:39.520 --> 00:38:40.940
study.

00:38:40.940 --> 00:38:45.350
Then we do the knockoff controlled variable
selection, or perhaps other variable selection,

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:47.100
it doesn't have to
be knockoff.

00:38:47.100 --> 00:38:52.170
And we apply multiple machine learning methods,
like the SuperLearner.

00:38:52.170 --> 00:39:00.590
Then we go ahead and rank mean square root
errors or accuracy metrics to identify how

00:39:00.590 --> 00:39:05.640
consistent are our
results, across different iterations.

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:09.520
And then in the final step, we do the feature
mining that allows us to identify

00:39:09.520 --> 00:39:18.940
these variables that are highly-- that appear
frequently, across the multiple random walks

00:39:18.940 --> 00:39:21.859
through the cloud of
data.

00:39:21.859 --> 00:39:26.480
So it is a very interesting algorithm, we
are right now in the process of validating

00:39:26.480 --> 00:39:28.220
it.

00:39:28.220 --> 00:39:30.730
So more on that later.

00:39:30.730 --> 00:39:36.240
But I want to point out some very important
things for us as a community, to look at the

00:39:36.240 --> 00:39:38.640
FAIR data and open
science principles.

00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:47.030
So FAIR refers to findable, A refers to accessible,
the I refers to interoperable, and R refers

00:39:47.030 --> 00:39:49.170
to
reusable data set.

00:39:49.170 --> 00:39:54.740
So as we all collect, manage, process, and
understand a complex data set we should always

00:39:54.740 --> 00:39:57.840
keep these four in mind, and I'm sure many
of us do.

00:39:57.840 --> 00:39:59.520
This is very important.

00:39:59.520 --> 00:40:06.359
And the open science aspects, again, not only
for the publications, but my group, the

00:40:06.359 --> 00:40:10.270
SOCR group, is actually using open continuous
development.

00:40:10.270 --> 00:40:12.720
All of these things that I'm showing to you,
the

00:40:12.720 --> 00:40:18.380
CBDA method, it's already on GitHub, even
before it's being published, sharing the data.

00:40:18.380 --> 00:40:23.660
All the data, the code,
and the science is being shared at the time

00:40:23.660 --> 00:40:24.810
of development.

00:40:24.810 --> 00:40:27.920
It's not shared after post hoc.

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:31.460
It is shared during the
process of development.

00:40:31.460 --> 00:40:38.020
So this obviously entices new investigators
to contribute, share, collaborate, extend,

00:40:38.020 --> 00:40:42.450
and so forth.

00:40:42.450 --> 00:40:46.980
So this final
example, I know we are approaching 12:50 here

00:40:46.980 --> 00:40:51.190
on the East Coast, and 9:50 on the West Coast,
here in the US.

00:40:51.190 --> 00:40:55.170
So the last thing I just want to show is an
area very interesting data set.

00:40:55.170 --> 00:41:01.150
Now in this case, about 60,000 patients,
and we looked at hospital admissions.

00:41:01.150 --> 00:41:06.540
The data is not very feature rich in this
case, only nine features are

00:41:06.540 --> 00:41:13.200
recorded, including admission, the length
of the admission, a binary indicator variable

00:41:13.200 --> 00:41:17.850
of whether the patient
survived, et cetera, and some demographics.

00:41:17.850 --> 00:41:25.220
We used very similar predictors trying to
identify, for instance, can we figure out

00:41:25.220 --> 00:41:28.970
who, at the time of admission, is
likely to die.

00:41:28.970 --> 00:41:33.490
So this confusion matrix that you have in
the bottom right here-- on the bottom left

00:41:33.490 --> 00:41:41.130
corner, illustrates
that the survivals are 9,900.

00:41:41.130 --> 00:41:47.500
It identifies the patients that-- the algorithm,
in this case, random forest, classified as

00:41:47.500 --> 00:41:51.050
patients that are expected to die, versus
how many did die.

00:41:51.050 --> 00:41:52.510
So we have this confusion matrix, and you
can see

00:41:52.510 --> 00:42:00.950
that we have a fairly decent internal cross-validation
accuracy of about 85%.

00:42:00.950 --> 00:42:03.360
The sensitivity is very high, about 93%.

00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:08.820
The specificity, however, is low, only 13%,
which indicates that we have a

00:42:08.820 --> 00:42:16.660
very, very low rate of falsely positively
identifying individual as expected die when

00:42:16.660 --> 00:42:22.420
they survived, but we might
have a fairly large rate of false negative

00:42:22.420 --> 00:42:23.470
identification.

00:42:23.470 --> 00:42:27.330
So people that did die, we might have missed
them.

00:42:27.330 --> 00:42:34.940
On the bottom right corner, we use this algorithm
to, on a completely independent data set of

00:42:34.940 --> 00:42:38.040
11,000 to 12,000
subjects, to essentially predict the probability

00:42:38.040 --> 00:42:39.250
of survival.

00:42:39.250 --> 00:42:40.480
What is that probability?

00:42:40.480 --> 00:42:44.720
We're not interested in binary
classifying individuals as surviving, we just

00:42:44.720 --> 00:42:49.859
want to have a likelihood which would help
physicians, clinicians and

00:42:49.859 --> 00:42:51.880
the caregivers.

00:42:51.880 --> 00:42:56.620
In addition-- so this is completely automated
decision support system that adds up another

00:42:56.620 --> 00:43:01.010
level of
quantitative description about likelihood

00:43:01.010 --> 00:43:05.090
of survival or not.

00:43:05.090 --> 00:43:14.770
So just to give you, again, coming back to
the National Health Service in the UK, we

00:43:14.770 --> 00:43:20.660
have begun the very same
exploratory data analytics using machine learning

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:23.010
methods on all of their data.

00:43:23.010 --> 00:43:26.830
We are developing these
workflows, we're looking at their genotypes.

00:43:26.830 --> 00:43:31.840
And ultimately, we're trying to design these
connectograms, that

00:43:31.840 --> 00:43:37.440
shows us, on the left, on the left hemisphere
here, you have some brain regions or clinical

00:43:37.440 --> 00:43:38.440
phenotypes.

00:43:38.440 --> 00:43:42.130
On the
right hemisphere, you had some of these genomic

00:43:42.130 --> 00:43:46.650
biomarkers, for instance.

00:43:46.650 --> 00:43:52.060
So finally, in the interest of time, I'm going
to skip this very interesting traumatic brain

00:43:52.060 --> 00:43:56.600
injury study, because I do
want to leave a few moments for questions.

00:43:56.600 --> 00:44:01.599
And the secret sauce of success are three
things, right.

00:44:01.599 --> 00:44:04.410
You have to
have the funding agencies, you have to have

00:44:04.410 --> 00:44:09.490
the collaborators, and you have to have the
infrastructure to do that

00:44:09.490 --> 00:44:10.680
type of study.

00:44:10.680 --> 00:44:15.380
So right up here, you have again, a recognition
of the funding agencies, as well as the

00:44:15.380 --> 00:44:17.790
corresponding grant IDs.

00:44:17.790 --> 00:44:23.380
Students, fellow, coworkers are very important
in this research.

00:44:23.380 --> 00:44:26.280
Big brain data and predictive health analytics
is a

00:44:26.280 --> 00:44:28.710
very transdisciplinary area.

00:44:28.710 --> 00:44:32.980
No one can do this individually, or even in
one lab.

00:44:32.980 --> 00:44:36.520
You have to have colleagues from
multiple disciplines from multiple institutions

00:44:36.520 --> 00:44:38.140
to do this kind of research.

00:44:38.140 --> 00:44:41.710
And of course, you have to have the
infrastructure, because without the computing

00:44:41.710 --> 00:44:45.330
resources, without the storage, without the
expertise, much of this

00:44:45.330 --> 00:44:48.710
research is going to be very difficult.

00:44:48.710 --> 00:44:49.710
So thank you very much.

00:44:49.710 --> 00:44:54.030
I'll be more than happy to address questions
now.

00:44:54.030 --> 00:44:59.900
Thank you so much, Ivo, that was an outstanding
tour of all these big data analytic projects

00:44:59.900 --> 00:45:01.020
that you've had going
on.

00:45:01.020 --> 00:45:07.090
One of the things which strikes me-- but before
I ask my question, I want to invite members

00:45:07.090 --> 00:45:10.530
of our audience, if
you have any questions, please, use the little

00:45:10.530 --> 00:45:14.390
GoToWebinar question submission system, which
is in that panel

00:45:14.390 --> 00:45:18.790
that's probably on the right side of your
screen, to type in your question and we'll

00:45:18.790 --> 00:45:20.140
get those.

00:45:20.140 --> 00:45:24.119
Text it in and then we
can ask those of Dr. Dinov.

00:45:24.119 --> 00:45:29.520
Ivo, how important is quality control, or
some level of quality assurance, of these

00:45:29.520 --> 00:45:32.530
variety of data sets that you're
pulling from?

00:45:32.530 --> 00:45:38.660
In particular was struck with all the different
publicly available governmental databases

00:45:38.660 --> 00:45:40.190
that you're
pulling from.

00:45:40.190 --> 00:45:45.550
Do you go through any quality assessment level
assessment of that data, to go and make sure

00:45:45.550 --> 00:45:49.160
that
it's valid and make some sense.

00:45:49.160 --> 00:45:52.600
And how do you look for outliers and that
sort of thing?

00:45:52.600 --> 00:45:55.270
Yeah, that's a very good question and a very
common question, Jack.

00:45:55.270 --> 00:45:59.690
And I'll tell you, for the dashboard example
that I illustrated with the CMS, Bureau of

00:45:59.690 --> 00:46:05.380
Labor Statistics, and the Census Bureau, we
did go through a very

00:46:05.380 --> 00:46:07.960
manual labor-intensive quality control.

00:46:07.960 --> 00:46:12.380
For instance, just to give you an example,
there were millions of records.

00:46:12.380 --> 00:46:15.500
And the federal government databases are full
of mistakes.

00:46:15.500 --> 00:46:18.780
About 3% to 5% of the data was wrong.

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:24.780
We were able to correct about 2% to 3% of
them, simply by crossvalidating,

00:46:24.780 --> 00:46:29.760
for example, things like FIPS codes and zip
codes and locale and what else.

00:46:29.760 --> 00:46:35.180
So there is mechanisms
that you can do, well, I don't know, semi-intelligent

00:46:35.180 --> 00:46:36.570
quality control.

00:46:36.570 --> 00:46:38.450
So that's on one level, right.

00:46:38.450 --> 00:46:40.990
So there are
mechanisms and you can do this.

00:46:40.990 --> 00:46:46.210
On a larger scale, however, this process is
not practical.

00:46:46.210 --> 00:46:48.590
And there is a danger, there is a danger that
if you collect

00:46:48.590 --> 00:46:54.690
the big data set, it could represent a very
substantially biased or non-representative

00:46:54.690 --> 00:46:55.920
data.

00:46:55.920 --> 00:46:57.930
That's always going to
be the case.

00:46:57.930 --> 00:47:05.390
Now the hope is that as we, collectively,
as community, improve the data quality, there

00:47:05.390 --> 00:47:09.340
will be
mistakes, but these mistakes on average, over

00:47:09.340 --> 00:47:13.400
the distribution of the data, they're going
to be representative of

00:47:13.400 --> 00:47:14.940
the population.

00:47:14.940 --> 00:47:21.619
So my personal approach is that we try to
do quality control the best we can, as long

00:47:21.619 --> 00:47:24.720
as it doesn't overwhelm us,
initially.

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:30.290
And secondarily, we are hoping and we are,
to some extent, validating whether or not

00:47:30.290 --> 00:47:33.760
the data is
representative of the census.

00:47:33.760 --> 00:47:38.089
And that's a difficult challenge, without
a doubt.

00:47:38.089 --> 00:47:41.349
So we have a question from one of our audience.

00:47:41.349 --> 00:47:45.170
And I was particularly interested, too, is
you were going to

00:47:45.170 --> 00:47:50.550
discuss how you used predictive analytics
in traumatic brain injury patients.

00:47:50.550 --> 00:47:55.480
And I was hoping to see a little more
description of that, before you skipped through

00:47:55.480 --> 00:47:56.480
it.

00:47:56.480 --> 00:47:57.619
And looks like somebody else from our audience
was, too.

00:47:57.619 --> 00:48:00.430
Can
you take a second, to just summarize that

00:48:00.430 --> 00:48:01.940
for us?

00:48:01.940 --> 00:48:03.450
Right, absolutely.

00:48:03.450 --> 00:48:06.680
So let me talk over this a little bit.

00:48:06.680 --> 00:48:11.670
And there will be a video, I hope, that plays
right.

00:48:11.670 --> 00:48:16.820
So traumatic
brain injury is incredibly interesting and

00:48:16.820 --> 00:48:18.480
incredibly challenging.

00:48:18.480 --> 00:48:22.710
The basic challenge is the heterogeneity of
the

00:48:22.710 --> 00:48:24.510
morbid condition, right.

00:48:24.510 --> 00:48:28.630
Everybody can imagine that every single traumatic
brain injury is different.

00:48:28.630 --> 00:48:34.510
So in this video here, we're illustrating
how we collected data from multiple sources,

00:48:34.510 --> 00:48:36.150
for multiple individuals.

00:48:36.150 --> 00:48:38.970
We
have access to the infrastructure, the pipeline

00:48:38.970 --> 00:48:43.730
computing server that you see, and the pipeline
graphical workflow

00:48:43.730 --> 00:48:45.770
environment.

00:48:45.770 --> 00:48:51.490
And now what we try to do, is we literally
individually process every single subject.

00:48:51.490 --> 00:48:55.380
So we parcelated
the brains, we looked at the morphometry metrics

00:48:55.380 --> 00:48:59.460
that we can automatically compute, for every
region, for every

00:48:59.460 --> 00:49:00.460
subject.

00:49:00.460 --> 00:49:02.890
We're able to reconstruct their cortical surfaces.

00:49:02.890 --> 00:49:08.930
And not only that, often times-- the injury
is very important, right.

00:49:08.930 --> 00:49:11.010
Is the injury the gray matter?

00:49:11.010 --> 00:49:15.760
Is it affecting in the myelinated white matter
tracts?

00:49:15.760 --> 00:49:17.700
How severe is it?

00:49:17.700 --> 00:49:19.520
Quantifying
some of these things, as well as the visual

00:49:19.520 --> 00:49:21.710
representation are very important.

00:49:21.710 --> 00:49:24.250
And TBI needs to be done on an
individual case.

00:49:24.250 --> 00:49:29.839
So look what's happening with this specific--
as this image is going to shift now a little

00:49:29.839 --> 00:49:34.880
bit, you can see that the
injury of this patient was in the inferior

00:49:34.880 --> 00:49:35.880
temporal lobe.

00:49:35.880 --> 00:49:40.270
Can everybody see the huge gap that we have,
the cavity

00:49:40.270 --> 00:49:43.820
that's indicative of exactly where the injury
is.

00:49:43.820 --> 00:49:44.820
How large is it?

00:49:44.820 --> 00:49:46.840
How complex is it?

00:49:46.840 --> 00:49:48.980
So these things are very
important.

00:49:48.980 --> 00:49:56.109
And then we try to look in this specific case,
we try to see if that type of injury, was

00:49:56.109 --> 00:49:58.860
a gunshot, motor
vehicle, or a bicycle accident?

00:49:58.860 --> 00:50:04.520
And unfortunately, we only have about 40 to
50 cases, in this case.

00:50:04.520 --> 00:50:10.430
We were trying to identify specific
commonalities.

00:50:10.430 --> 00:50:17.180
We did not have enough power to identify anything
critical, but the process that we went through

00:50:17.180 --> 00:50:21.230
was very powerful because we have built the
protocol.

00:50:21.230 --> 00:50:23.520
We know how to analyze these cases, we just
need

00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:33.460
additional, obviously, data and more processing
power to be able to obtain these on a larger

00:50:33.460 --> 00:50:37.230
scale, because
without hundreds to thousands of cases, the

00:50:37.230 --> 00:50:42.869
heterogeneity of TBI is really limiting the
scope of any method--

00:50:42.869 --> 00:50:49.089
model-based or model-free, to yield anything
substantially interesting.

00:50:49.089 --> 00:50:50.089
That's interesting.

00:50:50.089 --> 00:50:57.010
Ivo, we have a question from one of our audience,
asking about that in the big data era, this

00:50:57.010 --> 00:51:00.980
era
of gathering ever more larger data sets, one

00:51:00.980 --> 00:51:04.490
of the first things people seem to be interested
in doing is applying

00:51:04.490 --> 00:51:10.320
machine learning techniques, in order to be
able to make predictions about classifying

00:51:10.320 --> 00:51:14.859
subjects or being able to
make predictions about what something might

00:51:14.859 --> 00:51:17.099
be or whatnot.

00:51:17.099 --> 00:51:21.800
But the alternative is, can we make inferences?

00:51:21.800 --> 00:51:25.099
And how do we make inferences, even though
we can make

00:51:25.099 --> 00:51:27.460
these algorithms which do prediction?

00:51:27.460 --> 00:51:28.990
And is there a distinction?

00:51:28.990 --> 00:51:34.500
And how can we identify features or the relative
importance of some variables versus others?

00:51:34.500 --> 00:51:38.640
And that you're uniquely qualified to comment
on this, so I'd throw

00:51:38.640 --> 00:51:40.600
this back to you.

00:51:40.600 --> 00:51:44.170
Right, so that's a very interesting point.

00:51:44.170 --> 00:51:49.849
I want to start off by saying that data science
is recognized effectively, by

00:51:49.849 --> 00:51:55.931
many of us, as the fourth scientific pillar,
in addition to the experimental sciences is

00:51:55.931 --> 00:52:02.810
that started during, well, during
the Renaissance era, to the theoretical sciences

00:52:02.810 --> 00:52:04.470
that started in the 17th, 18th, and 19th century.

00:52:04.470 --> 00:52:09.920
The
computational sciences, which effectively

00:52:09.920 --> 00:52:13.530
matured in the 20th century, and in the 21st
century we have the data

00:52:13.530 --> 00:52:14.530
sciences.

00:52:14.530 --> 00:52:23.359
It is true that you can-- scientific discovery
can be hypothesis driven or exploratory.

00:52:23.359 --> 00:52:27.650
So this can be called
exploratory versus confirmatory science.

00:52:27.650 --> 00:52:29.520
Machine learning methods can be used for both.

00:52:29.520 --> 00:52:34.240
You can use machine
learning methods to formulate a research testable

00:52:34.240 --> 00:52:39.930
hypothesis that can be prospectively tested
or validated using

00:52:39.930 --> 00:52:40.930
other methods.

00:52:40.930 --> 00:52:45.930
But it can be used for inference as well,
simply because the statisticians in the past

00:52:45.930 --> 00:52:52.080
decade have
